Spanning Tree Protocol (STP): L2 protocol that allow us to have redunduncy in within a LAN. It's run on switches by default. It prevents L2 loops by placing 
redundant ports in a blocking state (disables the interface) instead of the regular forwarding state. Interfaces in a blocking state only send/receive STP messages. 
There's a set of processes to decide which ports are forwarding and which are blocking:

1. Interfaces connected to pc are put in forwarding
2. The switch with the lowest bridge ID (BID) will be the root. If they all have the default priority (32768), we will choose the one with the lowest MAC address. All
ports on the root bridge are designated ports (forwarding state).
3. The remaining switches will select one of its interfaces to be its root port, which is the one will the lowest root cost. The root port will be in a forwarding
state and the ports accross it will be designated. The root cost depends on the interface speed: 
10Mbps -> STP Cost of 100, 100Mbps -> STP Cost of 19, 1Gbps -> STP Cost of 4, 10Gbps -> STP Cost of 2. 
If many ports have the same root cost, we chose the one with the lowest neighbor switch ID, then if they also have same neighbor switch ID (in case we have 2 
connections between the switches) we check the lowest port ID on the neighbor.
4. For remaining collision domain (2 ports facing each other that we didnt touch yet), we select the device with the lowest root cost (even if it's another interface),
then the lowest bridge ID to be designated. The other one will be non-designated (blocking state).

There are 4 STP Port state: we previously covered forwarding and blocking which are stable states, but we also have listening and learning which are transitional states
that occur when some changes happen in the topology and an interface needs to change from blocking to forwarding. This is the process:

• Blocking state: drops user data frames, does not learn MAC addresses, and only receives Bridge Protocol Data Units (BPDUs) to map the topology and prevent loops. 
Lasts up to 20 seconds (Max Age timer) before transitioning if a redundant link must take over.

• Listening: still drops user frames and does not learn MAC addresses, but both sends and receives BPDUs as switches elect the root bridge and determine port roles. 
Lasts 15 seconds (Forward Delay timer).

• Learning: continues processing BPDUs and begins learning source MAC addresses to populate the MAC address table, but still does not forward user data frames. 
Lasts 15 seconds (Forward Delay timer).

• Forwarding: Actively forwards and receives user data frames, continues learning MAC addresses, and processes BPDUs on active root and designated ports.

STP Timers: they are time intervals used by the Spanning Tree Protocol (STP) in computer networking to control how switches exchange Bridge Protocol Data Units 
(BPDUs), transition ports between states, and prevent switching loops during topology changes. There's 3 types:

1. Hello timer -> 2s by default, it is the time it takes for the root bridge to send hello bdpus
